(defxdoc+ lambda-lifting-simulation
  :parents (lambda-lifting-validation)
  :short "The simulation induction for the validation of lambda lifting."
  :long
  (xdoc::topstring
   (xdoc::p
    "This book carries out the induction over the @(see
     eval-exprs/atoms/binds) clique for the validation of lambda lifting
     (see @(tsee lambda-lift-validation-claim-p)): if an expression
     evaluates in an environment, then what lifting rebuilds it into, in a
     context, evaluates, with some limit, in a related environment (see
     @(tsee expr-denv-ll-related-p)), to a related value (see @(tsee
     expr-value-ll-related-p)).  This is the soundness direction only:
     lifting may turn an evaluation that fails into one that succeeds.")
   (xdoc::p
    "The limit of the lifted evaluation differs from the original one:
     the reference that replaces a lifted lambda applies its definition to
     the variables the lambda captures, which takes evaluation steps that
     the original does not.  So the conclusions are existential in the
     limit, as well as in the witnesses of the relations; the evaluations
     of the parts of an expression are combined at the largest of their
     limits (see @(tsee eval-expr-of-larger-limit)).")
   (xdoc::p
    "As in @(see uniquify-evaluation), the statements are universally
     quantified predicates on the clique's own formals, one per function
     of the clique, so that the induction hypotheses can be instantiated
     at whatever the lifted side of each case is.  The statements about
     expressions, atoms, and bindings quantify over the context of the
     traversal and the lifted environment; those about values (the
     applications, the unboxing, and the higher-order primitives)
     quantify over the related lifted values.")
   (xdoc::p
    "The environment relation is stated at an expression.  For the
     statements about lists of expressions, atoms, and lists of atoms, it
     is stated at the bracket or array expression that consists of them,
     which lifting rebuilds into the bracket or array of their rebuilt
     forms; for bindings, at the @(':let') expression whose bindings start
     with them (see @(tsee let-or-body)), and their conclusions relate the
     environments at what remains of it.  The book proceeds bottom-up: these wrappings and the
     relations at sub-expressions, then one lemma per case of the
     evaluator, and finally the induction."))
  :order-subtopics t
  :default-parent t)

